# Tool-call rules for the guard engine. One rule per line:
#
#   tier | tool | pattern
#
#   tier     block, confirm, or allow. First match wins, so order matters and the
#            dangerous rules come first.
#   tool     the tool name to match, or * for any.
#   pattern  an extended regex matched against the tool's input, flattened to one line.
#
# The default for anything unmatched is set by the active oal-agent-profile, not here:
# scoped and trusted allow, untrusted confirms. That is what makes the posture a machine
# setting rather than a property of this file.
#
# Adding a rule: prefer confirm over block. A block with no override is a wall someone
# will eventually route around by disabling the hook entirely, which is worse than a
# prompt they answer. Reserve block for things with no legitimate agent use.
#
# Naming: no rule here names a runtime, for the same reason the engine does not. Where a
# vendor's file has to be covered, it is matched by shape (a settings file under a dotted
# directory in $HOME) rather than by brand, so a rule written for one agent covers the next.

# --- the terms the agent is judged by -------------------------------------------------
# These come first because every rule below is optional if any of them can be reached.
# Raising the posture to trusted turns every confirm in this file into an allow, so the
# command that does it, and the file it writes, have no legitimate agent use at all.
# Reading the current posture (oal-agent-profile with no argument) stays free.
block | * | oal-agent-profile[[:space:]]+(trusted|scoped|untrusted)
block | * | oal-guard-confirm
block | * | state/oal/(agent-profile|confirm|audit)
# Where a runtime is told which hook to run before a tool call. Matched by shape, not brand.
confirm | * | /\.[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*/settings\.json

# --- no legitimate agent use, no override ---------------------------------------------
block | * | rm[[:space:]]+(-[a-zA-Z]*[rR][a-zA-Z]*[[:space:]]+)+/([[:space:]]|$)
block | * | mkfs(\.[a-z0-9]+)?[[:space:]]
block | * | dd[[:space:]].*of=/dev/(sd|nvme|vd|hd)
block | * | :\(\)[[:space:]]*\{.*\|.*&.*\}
block | * | >[[:space:]]*/dev/(sd|nvme|vd|hd)[a-z]
block | * | chmod[[:space:]]+-R[[:space:]]+777[[:space:]]+/([[:space:]]|$)
block | * | oal-system-factory-reset

# Piping a downloaded script straight into a shell. The agent can download it, read it,
# and then run it -- that is the whole point of having an agent.
block | * | (curl|wget)[[:space:]][^|]*\|[[:space:]]*(sudo[[:space:]]+)?(ba|z|d|k)?sh

# --- credentials: readable by a person, never by an agent unattended -------------------
# Tool * rather than the structured read tool alone. These were scoped to it, which meant
# the rule covered the one path an agent does not have to use: head, less, base64, xxd,
# sed, awk, python -c and node -e all reached the same bytes and matched nothing, while
# the README said every posture refuses to read private keys and dotenv files.
#
# The cost of tool * is that a Write whose *content* mentions one of these paths is also
# refused. That is the right way round -- a false refusal is visible and one keystroke to
# work around, a false permit is neither -- but if it turns noisy, the tier is the thing
# to lower, not the tool.
block | * | /\.ssh/(id_(rsa|ed25519|ecdsa|dsa)([^.]|$)|authorized_keys)
block | * | \.env($|[^.a-zA-Z])
block | * | \.age($|")
block | * | /\.(netrc|npmrc|pypirc)($|[^.a-zA-Z])
block | * | /\.aws/credentials
block | * | /\.docker/config\.json
block | * | /\.gnupg/
block | * | /\.config/gh/hosts\.yml
block | * | /kwalletd/

# --- reversible but expensive, or hard to undo ----------------------------------------
confirm | * | \bsudo\b
confirm | * | pacman[[:space:]]+-(R|S[^y]|Syu)
confirm | * | systemctl[[:space:]]+(disable|mask|stop)
confirm | * | git[[:space:]]+push[[:space:]].*--force
confirm | * | git[[:space:]]+(reset[[:space:]]+--hard|clean[[:space:]]+-[a-z]*f)
confirm | * | (npm|pnpm|yarn|pip|uv)[[:space:]]+(install|add)[[:space:]]
confirm | * | \brm[[:space:]]+-[a-zA-Z]*[rR]
# Tool * for the same reason as the credential block above: these were scoped to the two
# structured write tools, so a shell redirect and every MCP server's write tool went past.
confirm | * | /etc/
